"prior_margin": 19.67, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": 21.5, "poll_sd": 6.17, "poll_weight": 0.4, "fundamentals": {"margin": 25.18, "lean": 14.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": 3.0, "money": 0.8}, "rating_margin": 16.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.84, "baseline_margin": 20.4, "analyst_adjustment": 0.6, "key_factors": ["Massachusetts' strong Democratic lean (D+14 PVI) with an incumbent seeking re-election", "Consistent polling shows Markey leading by roughly 19-23 points, most recently 53-30", "All ten expert ratings place the race as Solid or Safe Democratic", "Prediction markets price Markey at 98-99% to win"], "rationale": "Markey cleared a primary challenge from Seth Moulton and enters the general with a large fundraising edge, no outside opposition spending, and a polling average near 21 points. Every rater and all three prediction markets treat the race as decided, so the forecast stays firmly in Safe Democratic territory.", "watch": "A major late-breaking scandal or an unusually low-turnout electorate could shave a few points off the margin, though not the outcome.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ed Markey (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Ed Markey, first elected in 2013, is seeking re-election against Republican John Deaton in a state that leans about fourteen points Democratic. Markey survived a primary challenge from Seth Moulton centered on generational change, and now faces a general-election opponent who has raised about $1.6 million to Markey's $5.4 million.\n\nPolling throughout 2026 has been remarkably stable, with Markey leading by 19 to 25 points in every survey, including a 53-30 result in late September. Expert raters unanimously call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets give Markey roughly a 98 to 99 percent chance. The main open question is the size of the margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9975, "margin": 19.63}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0